    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "the \"sticky trap\" thing makes sense but how does it actually happen? like how does the LDL just decide to stay in the artery and start building up instead of just flowing through?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The LDL particles, when there are too many of them, can get stuck to the inner lining of your arteries, which are like the smooth tunnels that carry blood. Think of it like a tiny, persistent crowd of little sticky things trying to wedge themselves into the smooth walls of a narrow path. These particles can become oxidized—meaning they get damaged—and this damage is what signals the body to start building up fatty deposits, which is the plaque. This plaque hardens the artery walls, making them less flexible and narrower, which is the real danger for your heart.",
      "source": "target",
